<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koblenz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koblenz is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, a point marked by the Deutsches Eck monument. Founded as the Roman fort of Confluentes, it became a residence of the archbishop-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, and in 1791–1792 it sheltered French émigré princes. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1815 it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koblenz.jpg" alt="Koblenz" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger Weinandt · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_Stolzenfels_01_Koblenz_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Koblenz lies in a narrow flood plain between high hill ranges, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> meets the Middle Rhine. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> ad confluentes, meaning at the confluence. The meeting point of the rivers is the German Corner, which carries an equestrian statue of Emperor William I as a symbol of the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. On the opposite bank of the Rhine the hill of Ehrenbreitstein bore early fortifications around 1000 BCE and is crowned today by a citadel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 55 BCE Roman troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and built a bridge between Koblenz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andernach">Andernach</a>. The military post Castellum apud Confluentes was one of those set up by Drusus. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> the city was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and became a royal seat, and Carolingian heirs met there to discuss what became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843, by which it passed to Lotharingia. A synod of 922 condemned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The Norsemen sacked the city in 882. In 1018 Emperor Henry II gave it to the archbishop-elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, whose successors held it until the end of the 18th century.</p><p>After the fall of the Bastille in 1789 the archbishop-elector, uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>, welcomed French royalist émigrés, among them the Count of Provence and the Count of Artois. The Prince of Condé formed an army of aristocrats there. In 1792 the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> began to march from Koblenz in an unsuccessful invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the French Revolutionary army took the city in 1794. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> of 1801 made it the capital of the French department of Rhin-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>. The Congress of Vienna assigned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and in 1822 it became the seat of government of the Prussian Rhine Province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Koblenz is a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>. The city was heavily bombed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and rebuilt, and it served as the seat of government of Rhineland-Palatinate from 1947 to 1950. The Rhine Gorge was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2002, with Koblenz marking its northern end. The city marked its 2,000th anniversary in 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Koblenz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After French armies occupied the left bank of the Rhine, local supporters of the Revolution in towns such as Bonn, <b>Koblenz</b>, Cologne and Aachen proclaimed a Cisrhenian Republic in September 1797, with the encouragement of General Lazare Hoche, who favoured a separate state allied to France.</blockquote><a href="/state/cisrhenian-republic">Cisrhenian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The Geroldseck family was divided by inheritance disputes, and after their line died out in 1634 the lordship passed to the lords of Cronberg and then, in 1711, to the counts of von der Leyen, who governed it from <b>Koblenz</b> and their Saar estates.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-hohengeroldseck">County of Hohengeroldseck</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts of Sayn appear in 1139 with a castle above Bendorf, near <b>Koblenz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sayn">County of Sayn</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the following centuries they built a territory along the Moselle from Trier to <b>Koblenz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-trier">Electorate of Trier</a></li><li><blockquote>The armistice of 1918 gave the Allies bridgeheads on the right bank of the Rhine, drawn as circles of 30 kilometres around <b>Koblenz</b> and Mainz.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-bottleneck">Free State Bottleneck</a></li><li><blockquote>In October 1923 armed groups seized town halls in Aachen, <b>Koblenz</b>, Trier and other towns, and a Rhenish Republic was proclaimed, with a provisional government in Koblenz led by Josef Friedrich Matthes and Hans Adam Dorten.</blockquote><a href="/state/rhenish-republic">Rhenish Republic</a></li></ul></section>
